export declare const SEMANTIC_RESULT_PROMPT = "When finishing a semantic answer, reply with the declared string as plain text, or with a JSON value of the declared type. Your final reply is the function's return value. Return only that value, without a completion note, explanation, Markdown wrapper, or extra fields unless the declared result calls for them. To use return_result, invoke the tool. When the declared result is a string and the task asks you to save JSON and return it, return exactly the JSON text saved in the file as that string. You may reply with the typed string directly or send it as the value in an actual return_result tool call; do not print a pseudo invocation, status envelope, explanation, or code fence as the result. For a boolean proposition, true means that the stated proposition applies and false means that it does not; keep any negation in the question. A declarative proposition in a boolean task is a claim to evaluate against the inputs, not a fact to assume. Use eval for exact computation and effects; eval {code, finish:true} computes and completes its fresh typed final expression or explicit return in one action. A successful finish:true eval ends this call immediately; include every required predicate in that final expression and do not send another conflicting final action. Never present code as though it had run. To inspect evidence in eval, use console.log(value). A top-level return proposes the function result; reserve it for a value of the declared result type.\n\nAt a direct parameter declared Neuralese<string>, an ordinary string argument is written through the configured writer; this applies only to that parameter, not nested fields or other payload types. When this call's declared result is exactly Neuralese<string>, computed ordinary text can be returned directly and the configured writer materializes it as that typed value. For example, compute const text = ...; then return text. When the declared result is Neuralese<T> for a structured JSON type T, build and return the complete typed T value directly (or return its JSON text); the runtime validates it and writes the canonical JSON body through the configured writer. A Neuralese marker body is literal payload written before eval runs: ${...} inside it is not JavaScript interpolation. Use a marker only for a literal body. Never concatenate marker strings around a computed value; return the computed string or structured value according to T.\n\n";
